    "result_text": "All 54 tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the direct level-computation rule with a points system that maps to a level, returning both `membershipPoints` and `membershipLevel`.\n\n**Points formula** (start at 0): +2 email present, +1 `namesakeCount == 0`, +2 household of 3+, +3 tenure over 365 days. **Level mapping**: 1 (0\u20131), 2 (2\u20133), 3 (4\u20135), 4 (6+).\n\nChanges:\n\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Replaced `membershipLevel(...)` with a `membershipPoints(owner)` method (the additive formula) plus a `membershipLevel(owner)` that maps points to a band. Added the `membershipPoints` `@Mapping`, and swapped the old level constants for point constants.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a persisted `householdSize` field (with getter/setter). Household size isn't derivable from a single owner at read time, so \u2014 mirroring the existing `namesakeCount` pattern \u2014 it's computed and stored at create time.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added `countHouseholdSize(householdId)` (existing members with the same `householdId` + 1 for the new owner) and set it during create.\n- **Schema files** (hsqldb / h2 / postgres / mysql) \u2014 Added the `household_size` column.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the read-only `membershipPoints` field and updated `membershipLevel` (now max 4, new description).\n\nThe household \"of 3 or more\" is keyed on the existing deterministic `householdId` (last name + postcode). For example, the third household member with an email and no namesakes scores 2+1+2 = 5 points \u2192 level 3, and a new owner can never reach level 4 since the only route to 6 points is tenure (Cp23/Cp39 confirm this).",
